        "original_id": "6f96734e-26f9-5687-8f9d-39ce70fe624d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Cox Point, Coor Crags, Garfield Glacier, Hull Bay, Marie Byrd Land, Erickson Bluffs\n- People: Richard E. Byrd\n- Organizations: United States Antarctic Service\n- Roles: Rear Admiral, American naval officer\n\nFacts:\n- Richard E. Byrd was born on October 25, 1888, and died on March 11, 1957.\n- Richard E. Byrd was a United States Navy rear admiral and an American naval officer who specialized in exploration.\n- The United States Antarctic Service observed and photographed Cox Point from aircraft during 1939 to 1941 under the leadership of Richard E. Byrd.\n- Cox Point is a rock headland on the southwest side of the terminus of Garfield Glacier, where Garfield Glacier discharges into Hull Bay on the coast of Marie Byrd Land.\n- Coor Crags are several rock crags located 3.5 nautical miles southeast of Cox Point in the northern part of the Erickson Bluffs near the coast of Marie Byrd Land.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
